1
00:00:00,249 --> 00:00:03,051
Put yourself at the moment before a fix
is handed off.

2
00:00:03,437 --> 00:00:05,517
How do you know it solves the right
problem?

3
00:00:06,244 --> 00:00:10,185
We will use a fixed regression test and
an independent human review.

4
00:00:10,639 --> 00:00:14,934
In this order service, the agent can
choose how to inspect and edit the

5
00:00:14,934 --> 00:00:16,954
validator inside the planned scope.

6
00:00:17,386 --> 00:00:21,067
Each transition in the workflow depends
on a visible result.

7
00:00:22,649 --> 00:00:27,004
Read the issue and write down the
expected answer for each example.

8
00:00:27,538 --> 00:00:32,200
One and five should be accepted. Zero
and negative one should be rejected.

9
00:00:32,609 --> 00:00:34,964
Use the zero case for the regression
test,

10
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:37,649
and keep the earlier cases in the suite.

11
00:00:38,114 --> 00:00:42,452
These fixed inputs give us something
concrete to check before and after the

12
00:00:42,452 --> 00:00:43,102
edit.

13
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:48,771
Run the suite with the regression in
place and the original code still

14
00:00:48,771 --> 00:00:49,421
present.

15
00:00:49,721 --> 00:00:53,008
Pause at the failure. It's the zero-case
assertion,

16
00:00:53,156 --> 00:00:56,920
and the validator returned true even
though we expected false.

17
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:02,377
An import error would tell a different
story. Capture the assertion and its

18
00:01:02,377 --> 00:01:03,027
revision.

19
00:01:03,384 --> 00:01:07,143
Now we have evidence that the new test
actually detects the defect.

20
00:01:08,874 --> 00:01:13,428
Before asking for the edit, agree on a
gate. The post-fix tests must pass,

21
00:01:13,712 --> 00:01:15,384
and the files must fit the task.

22
00:01:15,837 --> 00:01:20,870
If an assertion fails, inspect it. If
the diff includes an unrelated file,

23
00:01:21,087 --> 00:01:22,960
follow up before handing it to review.

24
00:01:23,562 --> 00:01:27,597
Keep the regression's expected result
fixed while trying a repair,

25
00:01:28,018 --> 00:01:31,370
so the passing result still answers the
original question.

26
00:01:33,164 --> 00:01:37,050
Run the same command again, this time on
the edited revision.

27
00:01:37,477 --> 00:01:41,652
The zero-case test now passes, along
with the three existing cases.

28
00:01:42,255 --> 00:01:45,306
Check the diff and save this output
beside the first run.

29
00:01:45,709 --> 00:01:50,578
You've just shown a behavior change: the
same test observed acceptance before the

30
00:01:50,578 --> 00:01:52,472
fix and rejection after it.

31
00:01:52,768 --> 00:01:56,204
The reviewer can now evaluate the
implementation and scope.

32
00:01:58,002 --> 00:02:01,108
Once the human author has opened the
training pull request,

33
00:02:01,461 --> 00:02:05,614
use G H P R checks to see what
continuous integration observed.

34
00:02:06,029 --> 00:02:09,216
This sample has two completed checks. In
your repo,

35
00:02:09,410 --> 00:02:12,693
check the required set and what each
result belongs to.

36
00:02:13,226 --> 00:02:18,099
If a required check is still pending,
stay at that gate until it finishes and

37
00:02:18,099 --> 00:02:19,537
you can inspect the result.

38
00:02:21,062 --> 00:02:25,150
Now hand over the issue reference, diff,
test command, both outputs,

39
00:02:25,458 --> 00:02:26,882
and the pull request checks.

40
00:02:27,314 --> 00:02:32,052
The reviewer opens the actual change and
records their own decision with GitHub

41
00:02:32,154 --> 00:02:33,507
CLI.

42
00:02:33,921 --> 00:02:38,679
Give them enough to question the result:
which assertion failed, what changed,

43
00:02:38,896 --> 00:02:40,294
and what was verified.

44
00:02:40,759 --> 00:02:44,571
That's the value of a deterministic
checkpoint in a workflow that also

45
00:02:44,571 --> 00:02:45,626
requires judgment.
